      "latency_ms": 2552.3,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 508,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and one of the most abundant and essential substances on Earth. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\nChemically, water is represented by the formula **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. This means every single molecule of water is made up of:\n*   **Two hydrogen atoms**\n*   **One oxygen atom**\n\nThese atoms are bonded together tightly. Water is famous for being the **\"Universal Solvent,\"** which means it can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This property is due to its chemical structure, which allows it to attract and break down salts, sugars, minerals, and gases.\n\n### 2. The Physical View\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states depending on the temperature:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard form we drink, swim in, and rain from the sky.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F). Uniquely, solid water is *less dense* than liquid water, which is why ice floats.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water boils at 100°C (212°F) or evaporates into the air.\n\n### 3. The Ecological & Geographical View\nWater covers roughly **71% of the Earth's surface**, primarily in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). It is constantly moving in a cycle known as the **water cycle** (evaporating into the atmosphere, condensing into clouds, and precipitating back down as rain or snow). \n\n### 4. The Biological View\nWater is the foundation of all known life. \n*   The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**.\n*   Cells—the building blocks of life—are mostly water; it provides the watery environment (cytoplasm) necessary for cellular chemical reactions to occur.\n*   Without water, living organisms cannot transport nutrients, regulate body temperature, or waste metabolic byproducts.\n\n**In short:** Water is a simple molecule of hydrogen and oxygen, but it is the literal lifeblood of our planet, shaping our geography, driving our weather,"
